# Defining words and state

## Colon definitions

```forth
: name ( stack -- effect )
  ... ;
```

`;` ends the definition. Use `recursive` (Gforth/Forth-2012) when the word must call itself by name from inside its own body.

## CREATE / DOES>

A defining word builds new words. Two stack effects matter: one for **calling the defining word**, one for **calling each created word** (after `does>`).

```forth
: cell-array  ( n "name" -- )       \ defining-time: reserve n cells
  create cells allot
  does>     ( i a-addr -- addr )     \ run-time of a created word
    swap cells + ;

10 cell-array readings                \ defines 'readings'
0 readings @ .                        \ access element 0
```

`does>` puts the address of the created word's data area on the stack before running the body.

## IMMEDIATE

Words marked `immediate` execute during compilation of the enclosing definition (`if`, `then`, `[char]`, custom macros). Their job is usually to emit code via `postpone`, `compile,`, or `,`. They must keep the compile-time stack balanced.

## Literals and execution tokens

- `[ … ] literal` — interpret `…` at compile time, compile its single-cell result as a literal.
- `sliteral` — compile a string literal from `( c-addr u )` already on the compile-time stack.
- `postpone word` — compile the compilation semantics of `word` (handles immediate and non-immediate correctly).
- `'  word` ( interpret ) and `['] word` ( compile ) push an **execution token** (xt) for `word`; run it with `execute`.

## VOCABULARY / FORTH (if present)

Switching vocabularies is implementation-specific. In portable ANS code, avoid relying on `search-order` tricks unless the project explicitly uses them.

## Arrays and strings in memory

Prefer allocated buffers with explicit size (`create`, `allot`, `char+`, `cell+`) over treating the **parameter stack** as an array. See `forth-anti-patterns.mdc`.
